1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,089
Lesson 10.7.

2
00:00:02,469 --> 00:00:03,984
The Z1T architecture.

3
00:00:05,117 --> 00:00:09,551
In September twenty twenty-six,
Extropic published Z1T, a

4
00:00:09,551 --> 00:00:14,375
transformer-like language model
designed for its Z1 pbit chip.

5
00:00:14,755 --> 00:00:16,952
It brings together everything
we saw in this unit.

6
00:00:17,332 --> 00:00:22,016
Today we take it apart: its architecture,
how the work is split between the

7
00:00:22,016 --> 00:00:26,264
chip and an FPGA, and what its
energy and latency tables really say.

8
00:00:27,383 --> 00:00:30,591
Z1T is small: four layers,
dimension five hundred

9
00:00:30,591 --> 00:00:34,061
twelve, a context of one
thousand twenty-four tokens.

10
00:00:34,441 --> 00:00:39,898
Attention is replaced by gated convolutional
attention, from the AFT family.

11
00:00:40,278 --> 00:00:42,265
Normalization is DyT.

12
00:00:42,645 --> 00:00:45,489
The MLP uses tanh-linear units.

13
00:00:45,869 --> 00:00:49,051
All projections are
sparse, with four inputs

14
00:00:49,051 --> 00:00:52,088
per output, and the weights use four bits.

15
00:00:53,217 --> 00:00:57,940
Each output pbit spends its
sixteen couplings on four input

16
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:02,904
values times four pbits, with a
four-bit encoding called dy4p.

17
00:01:03,284 --> 00:01:07,919
The Z1 chip has eight cores and two
hundred sixty-nine thousand five hundred

18
00:01:07,919 --> 00:01:12,554
sixty-eight pbits, sampling at more
than fifty megahertz and under one watt.

19
00:01:12,934 --> 00:01:16,167
The sampled projections
and DyT run on the chip.

20
00:01:17,283 --> 00:01:21,259
The rest runs on an FPGA: the
embedding, the arithmetic of

21
00:01:21,259 --> 00:01:25,304
convolutional attention, the
residuals, positional encoding

22
00:01:25,304 --> 00:01:28,663
and the vocabulary head
that produces the logits.

23
00:01:29,043 --> 00:01:31,587
It is disaggregated
inference: each operation

24
00:01:31,587 --> 00:01:33,734
goes to the device where it is native.

25
00:01:34,114 --> 00:01:37,871
The article mentions that the
FPGA could be swapped for a GPU

26
00:01:37,871 --> 00:01:41,134
or another accelerator,
though that was not measured.

27
00:01:42,250 --> 00:01:48,711
The energy estimate is
294.52 nanojoules per token:

28
00:01:48,711 --> 00:01:54,791
8.74 for sampling on Z1
and 285.78 for the FPGA.

29
00:01:55,171 --> 00:01:57,972
The thermodynamic chip is
less than three percent.

30
00:01:58,352 --> 00:02:02,028
Against an H100 at ten percent
utilization, the reported

31
00:02:02,028 --> 00:02:05,834
advantage is about one hundred
thirty-nine times; at fifty

32
00:02:05,834 --> 00:02:09,510
percent, twenty-eight; at one
hundred percent, fourteen.

33
00:02:09,890 --> 00:02:15,101
The Z1 layers on their own would be
hundreds or thousands of times more

34
00:02:15,101 --> 00:02:20,459
efficient: the bottleneck is the FPGA,
as Amdahl predicted in lesson 9.5.

35
00:02:21,583 --> 00:02:22,876
Now the fine print.

36
00:02:23,255 --> 00:02:28,009
The vocabulary head, the last dense layer
that produces the logits, is excluded

37
00:02:28,009 --> 00:02:32,462
from the headline figures; included,
the energy rises to about one hundred

38
00:02:32,462 --> 00:02:36,614
thirty-six microjoules per token,
some four hundred sixty times more.

39
00:02:36,994 --> 00:02:39,752
Data movement between
chip and FPGA and the

40
00:02:39,752 --> 00:02:42,252
number of chips are not counted either.

41
00:02:42,632 --> 00:02:47,933
The estimated latency is 58.8
microseconds per token, against one

42
00:02:47,933 --> 00:02:53,559
hundred two on a compiled H100, and
FPGA orchestration is most of it.

43
00:02:53,939 --> 00:02:57,974
All are batch-one projections, not
measurements of the complete system.

44
00:02:59,083 --> 00:03:05,244
From Z1T we learn a design recipe, the need
to count energy per device including the

45
00:03:05,244 --> 00:03:10,670
vocabulary head, and that the next
gain lies in reducing the digital glue.

46
00:03:11,050 --> 00:03:15,336
In lab twenty-nine you will make
your own energy-per-token projection.

47
00:03:16,450 --> 00:03:19,655
Common mistakes: quoting one
hundred thirty-nine times

48
00:03:19,655 --> 00:03:22,623
without saying it assumes
ten percent utilization.

49
00:03:23,003 --> 00:03:25,593
Ignoring the excluded vocabulary head.

50
00:03:25,973 --> 00:03:29,863
Confusing the advantage of the Z1
layers with that of the complete system.

51
00:03:30,243 --> 00:03:32,875
And treating projections as
if they were measurements.

52
00:03:33,983 --> 00:03:38,877
In summary, Z1T combines gated
convolutional attention, DyT,

53
00:03:38,877 --> 00:03:43,933
tanh-linear units and degree-four
projections with dy4p pbits.

54
00:03:44,313 --> 00:03:48,067
The chip does the sampling; the
FPGA, everything else, and it

55
00:03:48,067 --> 00:03:51,205
takes ninety-seven percent
of the estimated energy.

56
00:03:51,585 --> 00:03:54,798
The figures are promising
projections with important

57
00:03:54,798 --> 00:03:57,825
exclusions, the vocabulary
head first among them.

58
00:03:58,950 --> 00:04:02,362
To practice: compute the FPGA share.

59
00:04:02,742 --> 00:04:05,764
Recompute the one hundred
thirty-nine times advantage.

60
00:04:06,144 --> 00:04:09,070
Compute the factor the
vocabulary head adds.

61
00:04:09,450 --> 00:04:12,034
And explain which component
dominates latency.
